WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:08.470
There’s a moment in the game ‘Viewfinder’
that kind of… broke my brain.

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:14.130
Actually, there are lots of moments that broke
my brain — the game is… ludicrous.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:18.840
But the most mind-shattering instance came
right at the start, when I held up my first

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.519
photo… and walked right into it.

00:00:21.519 --> 00:00:27.330
And even though I knew this was a main component
of the game’s world, I still said to myself:

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:29.140
“There’s no way.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:34.090
This game won’t actually let me place a
photo anywhere, it would completely break…

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:35.860
well, everything.”

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:40.890
But the game did let me, it encouraged me,
demanded I keep smashing guidelines, keep

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:47.110
tearing up the manual — because Viewfinder
is set in a world that breaks all the rules.

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:52.699
Typically, fictional worlds strive for immersion:
limiting themselves to certain parameters

00:00:52.699 --> 00:00:56.410
— even if those limits are more fantastical
than reality.

00:00:56.410 --> 00:01:00.930
But there’s another kind of fictional world,
the kind that shatters the confines of the

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:01.930
status quo.

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:07.020
So say goodbye to gravity, say goodbye to
time, say goodbye to space, and prepare for

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:13.530
a voyage across realities with no restrictions...

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:17.549
So how does one navigate a world that breaks
the rules?

00:01:17.549 --> 00:01:21.799
Simple — you break the rules right back,
and beat it at its own game.

00:01:21.799 --> 00:01:27.410
The world of Viewfinder consists of gravity-defying
islands separated by gaps that are impossible

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:28.590
to cross.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:33.659
Impossible, that is, unless you line up a
photo of a bridge, and then simply…

00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:35.909
make your way to the other side.

00:01:35.909 --> 00:01:37.980
Confronted with a locked gate?

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:42.729
Just hold up a photo of an open gate and…
pass right through.

00:01:42.729 --> 00:01:47.000
The world of Viewfinder might shatter the
parameters we’re familiar with, but it has

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.370
its own internal conventions that you can
learn if you keep an open mind.

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:56.640
Anything in the background of a photo just
kind of… deletes whatever’s in front of

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:01.720
it, which can allow you to access walled off
areas that are otherwise unreachable.

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:07.130
And anything in the foreground becomes something
physical, a tangible cross-section of reality

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:12.890
you can walk through, thanks to some trick
of programming that, to me, borders on miraculous.

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:17.160
But it’s not just photos that this effect
works on.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:22.130
You can also enter and wander through the
colorful brushstrokes of a painting.

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:27.820
Or explore the cartoonish landscape of a postcard,
with the simple iconography transformed into

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:29.610
material objects.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:34.620
Even the scribbles of a child can become something
corporeal — something you feel you can reach

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:36.600
out and touch.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:38.730
It’s… bizarre at first.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:44.440
But the deeper you go into the world of Viewfinder,
the harder it is to remember things ever working

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:45.690
another way.

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:50.319
With each new layer, the rules of traditional
reality recede further and further into the

00:02:50.319 --> 00:02:55.320
background, and even as some things start
to seem off, you pay no attention, as if you’re

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.080
trapped in a dream and need to find a way
to —

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:00.959
(Wake up)
“— Our patent-pending somnasculpt technology

00:03:00.959 --> 00:03:09.625
provides safe and effective dream therapy
while you rest in the comfort of our flagship

00:03:09.625 --> 00:03:14.990
clinic —” The entirety of the 2019 game
Superliminal takes place within a dream.

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:20.269
That’s not a spoiler like it would be for
most pieces of dream-centric media.

00:03:20.269 --> 00:03:22.819
The fact you’re in a dream is obvious.

00:03:22.819 --> 00:03:25.659
What isn’t obvious is how to wake up.

00:03:25.659 --> 00:03:31.709
Like Viewfinder, Superliminal is a game that
asks you to break all the rules, and see reality

00:03:31.709 --> 00:03:34.220
from a new perspective.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:39.519
In this world, if you pick up an object and
walk backwards, said object will grow in accordance

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:40.900
with your changed viewpoint.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:46.250
It’s a feature of the dreamscape that on
paper makes little sense, yet somehow feels

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:47.630
completely logical.

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:53.030
And it also works in reverse: if an object
gets too big, you can shrink it down with

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:55.020
a simple perception shift.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:04:00.459
With this reality-bending ability, outsmarting
the puzzle-filled world seems like a cinch.

00:04:00.459 --> 00:04:04.700
But the world of Superliminal will bend reality
right back.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:09.860
The ultimate goal of the game is to escape
a labyrinthine dream facility by finding a

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:12.130
door to the waking realm.

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:17.829
Yet this is easier said than done, because
the facility is a maze of false exits.

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:22.310
One hallway that seemed to offer a way out
was actually a forced-perspective illusion,

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:25.550
with an exit too small to fit through.

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:30.350
Another wasn’t a hallway at all, but a camouflaged
sculpture meant to trick the eye.

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:35.680
Once, the actual way forward was painted to
look like a dead end, a deception that caused

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:39.650
me to stop trusting my perception altogether.

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:45.250
These illusions are examples of anamorphosis:
distorted projections that require the viewer

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:49.370
to occupy a specific vantage point to make
sense of them.

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:55.169
Like the puzzles of Viewfinder, they require
you to, literally, change your perspective,

00:04:55.169 --> 00:04:59.350
and embrace the strange new paradigm of the
world around you.

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:04.800
Indeed, many sections of Viewfinder feature
a similar anamorphosis mechanic.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:09.520
Yet notably, such illusions don’t just exist
in the virtual realm.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:15.000
Painted ceilings from the 1600s create depth
using analogous visual trickery.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:20.350
Those forced perspective tourist photos of
the Leaning Tower of Pisa fall into the same

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:21.350
category.

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:23.910
Such illusions are simple — even silly.

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:28.960
Yet though often played for laughs, there
can be something… disarming about convincing

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:31.020
optical illusions.

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:35.190
Like having the rug of reality pulled out
from under you.

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:40.620
Many films use oversized furniture and clever
angles to manipulate the viewer’s understanding

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:41.620
of scale.

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:45.910
It’s an effect that’s often meant to be
invisible — not a rug-pull, but a harmless

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:48.910
technique to create a convincing fictional
setting.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:53.990
What’s uncanny about such effects, however,
is that the spatial distortion is happening

00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:55.740
within your mind.

00:05:55.740 --> 00:06:00.800
Modern cameras aren’t required: these illusions
work simply because our brains expect the

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:07.490
world to follow certain rules, an expectation
that can be taken advantage of to, essentially,

00:06:07.490 --> 00:06:09.160
warp reality.

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:14.380
A broken rule in a virtual dream is one thing,
but a physical optical illusion can leave

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:19.910
you questioning the waking world, wondering
if the laws of reality are truly as consistent

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:25.160
as they appear.

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:31.560
In the world of Manifold Garden, the law of
gravity… is more of a suggestion.

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:36.571
Floors become ceilings, stairs become bridges,
there isn’t really an up or down, there

00:06:36.571 --> 00:06:40.440
just kind of… is.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:45.870
Created by American artist Willian Chyr, Manifold
Garden might be advertised as a modern puzzle

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:50.910
game, but the project’s primary inspiration
is the work of a Dutch mathematician born

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.210
in in 1898.

00:06:53.210 --> 00:07:00.590
The drawings of MC Escher are mazes of impossibility,
using early perspective illusions and gravity-defying

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:06.021
architecture to create landscapes that could
not exist… and yet Manifold Garden, through

00:07:06.021 --> 00:07:10.770
modern programing, makes these worlds explorable.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:15.440
In order for this to work, the geometry of
each landscape loops infinitely.

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:20.840
You can jump off any structure and know with
absolute certainty you will eventually return

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:23.740
to a place identical to where you started.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:30.180
As a result, falling — one of the most conventional
ways to lose in a game — just… isn’t

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:32.530
a concern here.

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:37.750
Falling is similarly deemphasized in Viewfinder,
as you can rewind time from any point if you

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:39.139
make a mistake.

00:07:39.139 --> 00:07:43.220
This serves as a reminder that traditional
rules aren’t what you should be focused

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:44.220
on.

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:49.319
But the lack of guidelines can also leave
you feeling somewhat untethered, even frustrated

00:07:49.319 --> 00:07:54.770
— like nothing you do here has any real
consequence.

00:07:54.770 --> 00:08:00.770
Navigating a world that breaks the rules can
be, often by design, aggravating Superliminal

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:06.319
is probably most upfront about its attempts
to provoke you, and notably, it frustrates

00:08:06.319 --> 00:08:12.069
you not just by removing guidelines, but by
creating illogical guidelines of its own.

00:08:12.069 --> 00:08:17.430
The game will frequently demand you follow
the exit signs located throughout the labyrinth,

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:21.550
yet many signs lead nowhere, or are utterly
incomprehensible.

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:26.449
At one point, if you trust the signs, you’ll
be trapped in a hallway that repeats forever.

00:08:26.449 --> 00:08:32.640
The only way to actually progress is to pick
up an exit sign, and use it as a ramp to escape

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:36.370
— outwitting the dream with its own underhanded
logic.

00:08:36.370 --> 00:08:42.310
Yet Superliminal will even break conventions
during moments like loading screens —periods

00:08:42.310 --> 00:08:44.310
most players might consider a safe-zone.

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:49.500
At different points in the game, the loading
screen will mutate, with the progress bar

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:55.440
sometimes jittering backwards, folding through
3D space, or expanding beyond its typical

00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:00.310
borders — in one of the more literal moments
of fourth-wall boundary-pushing I’ve seen

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:01.640
in media.

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:06.200
Even the title doesn’t obey instructions,
collapsing to the floor if you click on it:

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:09.060
another rug-pull in a game constantly pulling
rugs.

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:11.440
It can make you feel like you’re in freefall.

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:13.500
Why don’t the laws make sense?

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:15.690
Who is in charge of this place?

00:09:15.690 --> 00:09:20.450
And why is this rabbit hole so deep?

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:26.769
Lewis Carroll’s 1865 novel “Alice in Wonderland”
tells the story of a girl who falls through

00:09:26.769 --> 00:09:32.410
a rabbit hole into a land of illogical characters
and nonsensical scenarios.

00:09:32.410 --> 00:09:38.370
It is also, perhaps unsurprisingly, absolutely
foundational to the concept of worlds that

00:09:38.370 --> 00:09:39.880
break all the rules.

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:43.470
In Wonderland, signposts give misleading advice.

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:48.800
Sizes shift unexpectedly, brought to life
in early film adaptations through — how

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:49.800
else?

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:51.269
— forced perspective.

00:09:51.269 --> 00:09:56.161
The DNA of the story can be felt in pretty
much every piece of media in this video: from

00:09:56.161 --> 00:09:59.660
little references to full on character homages
(aa-muhj).

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:04.450
Viewfinder even has its own version of the
Cheshire Cat — a figure that offers cryptic

00:10:04.450 --> 00:10:08.120
advice about how to navigate an illogical
world.

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:13.870
Because critically, Wonderland is deeply frustrating
for Alice to traverse, and at times even a

00:10:13.870 --> 00:10:15.470
little sinister.

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:21.149
In many ways, it feels like the world of adults
from a child’s perspective: chaotic, threatening,

00:10:21.149 --> 00:10:24.990
and governed by rigid conventions that make
little sense.

00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:29.959
The world seems a meaningless puzzle, made
worse by attempts at policies, occupied by

00:10:29.959 --> 00:10:34.550
people who don’t realize the absurdity of
their own lives…

00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:39.709
It kind of reminds me of going to the airport.

00:10:39.709 --> 00:10:46.839
The 2014* absurdist adventure game Jazzpunk
opens not with a bang, but with the slow procession

00:10:46.839 --> 00:10:49.089
of an airport baggage claim.

00:10:49.089 --> 00:10:54.070
The luggage leisurely inches along as the
name of the game’s studio hovers silently

00:10:54.070 --> 00:10:58.600
in the air like a signpost for a boarding
gate, and then…

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:03.660
(music)
The game just absolutely loses it.

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:11.769
With zero buildup or justification, it launches
into a nearly two minute, James-Bond-style

00:11:11.769 --> 00:11:17.030
intro sequence — the old-school espionage
imagery alluding to the Cold War spy genre

00:11:17.030 --> 00:11:18.269
the game parodies.

00:11:18.269 --> 00:11:24.160
But it just keeps going and going, the sense
of importance rising, the energy swelling,

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:26.930
until it throws you, spinning…

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:29.880
into an extremely dull office space.

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:33.020
From here, the game becomes absurdly realistic.

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:39.529
Though you play as an international superspy,
you have to sit down and actually peruse articles

00:11:39.529 --> 00:11:42.600
as you wait in real time for your boss to
be ready.

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:47.889
When the waiting’s finally over, your boss
tells you to infiltrate Soviet intelligence,

00:11:47.889 --> 00:11:56.270
then proclaims he’ll be ‘in his wine cellar’
before pretending to walk… below his desk.

00:11:56.270 --> 00:11:59.089
Jazzpuuuuuunk!

00:11:59.089 --> 00:12:04.490
Like most works of satire, Jazzpunk points
out absurdity: not just with a genre, but

00:12:04.490 --> 00:12:06.810
with various aspects of reality.

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:12.470
The world it takes place in — a retro-futuristic,
alternate history version of the United States

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:17.600
in the late 1950s, is suffocating under suspicion
and bureaucracy.

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:23.470
Yet though most characters treat this atmosphere
of espionage extremely seriously, their attempts

00:12:23.470 --> 00:12:26.540
at counterintelligence feel profoundly unserious.

00:12:26.540 --> 00:12:32.029
There’s an agent hiding in a mailbox who
tells you to go away, a milk van following

00:12:32.029 --> 00:12:37.370
you around with a completely unsuspicious
amount of radio equipment, a senior officer

00:12:37.370 --> 00:12:42.020
who uses top-secret carrier pigeons to…
bake a pie.

00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:47.930
Though gravity never inverts and space never
warps in Jazzpunk, through its parody of social

00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:53.949
conventions, its world feels just as absurd,
just as boundary-breaking, as any other discussed

00:12:53.949 --> 00:12:55.160
so far.

00:12:55.160 --> 00:12:59.230
But there’s one moment in particular that
feels…

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:00.230
different.

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:05.380
Early in the game, you can sit in a movie
theater and watch a never-ending loop of a

00:13:05.380 --> 00:13:08.250
commercial for something called a ‘Swing
Wing.’

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:14.320
And though the product seems too absurd, too
pointless to actually exist… the Swing Wing

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:18.060
is, in fact, a real toy from 1965.

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:24.620
One that was, unsurprisingly, discontinued
for giving children concussions.

00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:29.959
The game’s characters watch in silent rapture,
as if nothing in their parody universe can

00:13:29.959 --> 00:13:34.690
match the preposterousness of our reality.

00:13:34.690 --> 00:13:40.079
During the height of Cold War paranoia, the
CIA launched project ‘Acoustic Kitty.’

00:13:40.079 --> 00:13:47.330
The plan, which is real, was to spy on enemy
embassies using a radio transmitter implanted

00:13:47.330 --> 00:13:48.620
within a cat.

00:13:48.620 --> 00:13:53.970
The plan failed due to the shocking revelation
that cats don’t like following rules.

00:13:53.970 --> 00:13:56.870
It cost twenty million dollars.

00:13:56.870 --> 00:14:02.019
Around the same time, British defense came
up with project Blue Peacock, a plan to detonate

00:14:02.019 --> 00:14:07.660
a nuclear warhead in the event of a Soviet
invasion on an eight-day delayed timer.

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:12.320
The timer was to be set off by a live chicken
sealed within the warhead itself.

00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:17.639
When the plan was declassified, many assumed
it was an April Fool’s joke.

00:14:17.639 --> 00:14:23.160
The official government response was, and
I quote, “The Civil Service does not do

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:24.160
jokes.”

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:29.491
And it’s after learning things like this
that the world of Jazzpunk doesn’t seem

00:14:29.491 --> 00:14:31.240
so absurd.

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:36.889
The fact that buildings have both a fire alarm
and an ‘ice alarm,’ starts to feel only

00:14:36.889 --> 00:14:40.580
a few degrees removed from the silliness of
real life.

00:14:40.580 --> 00:14:45.089
This is a video about fictional worlds that
break all the rules, but sometimes reality

00:14:45.089 --> 00:14:48.040
feels just as lawless and arbitrary.

00:14:48.040 --> 00:14:56.070
Okay, now it’s time to talk about An Airport
for Aliens Currently Run by Dogs.

00:14:56.070 --> 00:15:02.820
Released in 2021, the game takes place in,
well: an airport for aliens, currently run

00:15:02.820 --> 00:15:04.180
by dogs.

00:15:04.180 --> 00:15:09.300
As a result, the familiar conventions of an
airport are filtered through two degrees of

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:10.300
distortion.

00:15:10.300 --> 00:15:16.430
The dogs, (or weird, floating jpegs of dogs)
who run the airport, have made various, canine-specific

00:15:16.430 --> 00:15:17.430
modifications.

00:15:17.430 --> 00:15:22.830
The bar serves toilet water, the bathrooms
are full of fire hydrants — you get it.

00:15:22.830 --> 00:15:27.860
Due to dogs’ poor short-term memory, if
you ask for a boarding pass, you might get…

00:15:27.860 --> 00:15:28.860
fifty.

00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:33.970
Yet even more challenging is the other layer
of distortion: since this is an airport for

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:37.750
aliens, all the signage is in an unreadable
language.

00:15:37.750 --> 00:15:43.350
And the game does actually expect you to navigate
to your flight on time, a process that can

00:15:43.350 --> 00:15:47.639
make you long for the rules of a real airport.

00:15:47.639 --> 00:15:52.960
But here’s something that playing An Airport
for Aliens Currently Run by Dogs helped me

00:15:52.960 --> 00:15:53.960
realize.

00:15:53.960 --> 00:15:55.649
All airports are fictional.

00:15:55.649 --> 00:16:01.380
And by fictional I don’t mean they don’t
exist — you can, of course, go to one.

00:16:01.380 --> 00:16:06.220
But everything about an airport, from the
frustrating baggage claim, to the endless

00:16:06.220 --> 00:16:10.990
signs, to the dividing barriers, are, ultimately,
something invented.

00:16:10.990 --> 00:16:15.199
A kind of collective fiction of procedures
and rules we all buy into.

00:16:15.199 --> 00:16:19.699
And I’m not saying all these procedures
are meaningless — if a pilot decided to

00:16:19.699 --> 00:16:24.509
stop following the lines of a runway, there’d
obviously be consequences.

00:16:24.509 --> 00:16:29.199
But the lines of a runway have no inherent
meaning, only the meaning we’ve assigned

00:16:29.199 --> 00:16:30.769
to them.

00:16:30.769 --> 00:16:35.630
Sometimes it’s only possible to recognize
the arbitrary nature of these invented places

00:16:35.630 --> 00:16:41.440
when we see them from a new perspective, like
when they’re empty or, you know, run by

00:16:41.440 --> 00:16:42.440
dogs.

00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:47.330
It’s bewildering when a fictional world
breaks with the rules of reality, but many

00:16:47.330 --> 00:16:53.470
of the rules we follow in reality are their
own kind of fiction.

00:16:53.470 --> 00:16:55.500
Does it sound like I’ve lost my mind?

00:16:55.500 --> 00:16:56.740
A little bit?

00:16:56.740 --> 00:17:01.990
It’s hard to stay grounded when exploring
these worlds, particularly the very last one

00:17:01.990 --> 00:17:03.959
I want to look at for this video.

00:17:03.959 --> 00:17:10.819
Cuccchi (coo-key) is… not really a game,
not really a movie, um… it exists.

00:17:10.819 --> 00:17:16.730
Cuccchi is made up of simple icons floating
in a void, and if you steer towards one, it

00:17:16.730 --> 00:17:21.140
will be placed into the context of a surreal,
painterly environment.

00:17:21.140 --> 00:17:26.439
Many of these landscapes are about as abstract
as they could possibly be without being completely

00:17:26.439 --> 00:17:32.970
untraversable, with some made up of just a
few flat images in a completely white abyss.

00:17:32.970 --> 00:17:38.730
Each region is inspired by a different painting
by Italian artist Enzo Cucchi, hence the title

00:17:38.730 --> 00:17:39.820
of the game.

00:17:39.820 --> 00:17:46.300
Cucchi’s paintings are, similarly, enigmas
— a maze of doubt and tangled interpretation.

00:17:46.300 --> 00:17:51.630
Yet what’s most interesting about Cuccchi
— the digital world, not the man — is

00:17:51.630 --> 00:17:55.850
how rarely I felt lost when navigating each
environment.

00:17:55.850 --> 00:18:01.280
Even when things were at their most abstract,
I could always find a way to orient myself,

00:18:01.280 --> 00:18:06.429
latch on to some familiar iconography and
watch as the landscape took shape.

00:18:06.429 --> 00:18:12.110
In many respects, Cuccchi feels like ultimate
proof of how few rules we need to stay grounded,

00:18:12.110 --> 00:18:18.200
how our brains can conjure a full-fledged
world from the simplest 2D projections.

00:18:18.200 --> 00:18:22.760
Exploring the realms of Cuccchi made me reevaluate
how I think about some of the other pieces

00:18:22.760 --> 00:18:25.820
of media I’ve discussed thus far.

00:18:25.820 --> 00:18:30.970
I think there’s real significance to the
fact that the most frustrating fake exit in

00:18:30.970 --> 00:18:33.600
Superliminal… is also the simplest.

00:18:33.600 --> 00:18:38.370
It’s just a painting of the sky propped
up at the right angle to look like the real

00:18:38.370 --> 00:18:39.370
thing.

00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:45.060
And while your annoyance at being Wile E Coyote’d
is still fresh, the game offers a ‘Relaxation

00:18:45.060 --> 00:18:46.060
Room.’

00:18:46.060 --> 00:18:51.179
Inside is a projection of a patch of sky — the
same patch of sky that all the fake exits

00:18:51.179 --> 00:18:53.270
in the game are modeled off of.

00:18:53.270 --> 00:18:58.539
It’s emotionally overwhelming knowing that
the goal of the game is right there — but

00:18:58.539 --> 00:19:00.530
it’s just a 2D illusion.

00:19:00.530 --> 00:19:06.230
But here’s the thing — everything about
the world of Superliminal is a 2D illusion.

00:19:06.230 --> 00:19:08.960
The same is true for the world of Viewfinder.

00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:13.780
Both from an in-universe perspective: everything
in Superliminal is a dream, and everything

00:19:13.780 --> 00:19:17.100
in Viewfinder is — well, I won’t give
that away here.

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:23.130
But also, in the most literal way possible:
these worlds are flat renders on computer

00:19:23.130 --> 00:19:24.130
screens.

00:19:24.130 --> 00:19:29.309
No aspect of them has any more real-world
depth than another, the fake exits are no

00:19:29.309 --> 00:19:30.990
different from the real ones.

00:19:30.990 --> 00:19:36.090
The same holds true for all fictional worlds
in visual mediums, whether they break the

00:19:36.090 --> 00:19:37.670
rules or not.

00:19:37.670 --> 00:19:42.530
Every film you’ve ever seen is an illusion:
essentially a series of photographs played

00:19:42.530 --> 00:19:47.240
in quick succession to break your brain into
thinking that subject is moving.

00:19:47.240 --> 00:19:52.650
There are hand-drawn disks from the 1800s
that basically work the same way, deceiving

00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:56.090
you into seeing motion when spun at the right
speed.

00:19:56.090 --> 00:20:02.830
The photographs of Viewfinder, the false exits
of Superliminal — these feel uniquely illusory.

00:20:02.830 --> 00:20:09.560
But from a certain point of view, all fiction
is its own type of illusion.

00:20:09.560 --> 00:20:16.150
To return a final time to “Alice in Wonderland,”
as the novel wears on, the Victorian-era rationality

00:20:16.150 --> 00:20:21.260
that adults have taught Alice consistently
fails in an inherently chaotic world.

00:20:21.260 --> 00:20:26.330
It is only in embracing the irrationality
of Wonderland, in accepting that the rules

00:20:26.330 --> 00:20:32.581
are nonsensical and can be broken, that Alice
can return home — or, more accurately, wake

00:20:32.581 --> 00:20:34.929
up from a dream.

00:20:34.929 --> 00:20:39.170
Sometimes the value of worlds that shatter
conventions is in waking you up to the ways

00:20:39.170 --> 00:20:41.380
reality doesn’t make sense.

00:20:41.380 --> 00:20:46.240
So many things in our daily routines are arbitrary,
the result of looking at the world from a

00:20:46.240 --> 00:20:48.799
particular point of view.

00:20:48.799 --> 00:20:51.799
Sometimes that can be comforting, or even
useful.

00:20:51.799 --> 00:20:56.549
But sometimes, it’s worth changing your
perspective.

00:20:56.549 --> 00:20:59.080
As always, thanks for watching.

00:20:59.080 --> 00:21:03.600
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support by liking, subscribing, and hitting

00:21:03.600 --> 00:21:08.309
the notification icon to stay up to date on
all things Curious.

00:21:08.309 --> 00:21:09.520
See you in the next video.

